course: everyday
lesson: 60
title: Thinking cleverly about people
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: People act from what is going on inside them, and you only see the outside: a person who snaps at you may be tired, worried or hurting, so a calm, kind answer often helps more than snapping back, though rudeness is never something you have to accept forever.
explain: Fair does not always mean everyone gets exactly the same; fair means everyone gets what they need to have a real chance, like glasses for a child who cannot see the board.
explain: Sharing and taking turns are not only kind but clever: a game where one player keeps the ball soon has nobody left to play with, while a game everyone enjoys keeps going.
explain: To tell a good secret from a bad one, ask: will it be told soon and make someone happy, like a surprise party, or must it be kept forever and does it give me an «uh-oh» feeling? The second kind you always tell a trusted adult.
explain: Clever fairness tricks exist: when two children share a cake, one cuts and the other chooses, so the cutter has every reason to make the pieces equal.
explain: A girl in Texas trusted a new online friend because he knew her school and hobbies; he had simply read her public posts, and he used them to trick her into thinking he knew her.
explain: A boy in Sydney believed a rumour about a classmate and spread it; it was false, and the classmate was left out for weeks before the truth came out.
fact: You can see what people do, but not always why they do it.
fact: People who are tired, worried or sad are more easily grumpy.
fact: Fair means everyone gets what they need, which is not always the same thing.
fact: A game only keeps going if the players are happy to keep playing.
fact: A surprise ends soon and makes someone happy; a bad secret is meant to be kept forever.
fact: A secret that makes you feel worried or uncomfortable should be told to a trusted adult.
fact: When one person cuts and the other chooses, the cutter has a reason to cut fairly.
fact: A rumour can be untrue, and even a true one can hurt.
fact: Someone who is quiet may be shy or new, not unfriendly or «stuck up» (thinking he is better than others).
fact: Grown-ups can be wrong, and some lie, just like children; being older does not make something true.
fact: A real apology owns up to what you did without an excuse.
fact: Winning by cheating does not show who is really better.
fact: Taking turns going first is fair when going first gives an advantage.
fact: Spreading a rumour can hurt someone badly even when it turns out to be false.
fact: Someone knowing facts about you does not prove they are a friend.
exercise: A cashier is short with your mum after a long queue. What is the wisest way to think about it? | She may be tired after a hard day, so a polite answer helps most | She must dislike your family, so be rude back to her and show her how it feels | Everyone who is short with you is a mean person | It proves your mum did something wrong
exercise: Only one child in class gets to use a special cushion because of a bad back. Is this unfair? | No, fair means everyone gets what they need | Yes, everyone should get a cushion or nobody | Yes, because the rule must be the same for all | No, because she asked the teacher nicely first
exercise: Two children share the last piece of pizza. Which way makes cheating pointless? | One cuts it and the other chooses first | The older child cuts it and then chooses first | Whoever is hungrier cuts it and chooses too | They ask a friend who secretly likes one of them
exercise: Why does a football game with one ball fall apart if one player never passes? | The others stop having fun and stop playing | The ball gets tired of the same feet | The player who keeps it scores every single time, so the others lose | Games are always better with one star
exercise: Which is a good secret to keep? | Helping Dad plan a surprise party for Mum on Saturday | An adult says you must never tell anyone about your games together | A friend says she is being hurt at home and not to tell | Someone online asks you to keep your chats secret
exercise: Why should a «forever» secret that gives you an uh-oh feeling be told? | A secret like that often hides something that is unsafe | Because all secrets must be told at once | Because keeping secrets is always naughty | Because adults are owed every single secret that a child knows
exercise: You hear a nasty rumour about a classmate. Why should you not pass it on, even if it might be true? | It could be untrue, and even if true it would hurt | Because rumours are only ever bad when they turn out to be false | Because nobody will believe you anyway | Because it is only wrong if teachers hear it
exercise: A new girl in Anchorage does not talk to anyone for a week. What is the most sensible guess? | She may be shy or finding it hard being new | She thinks she is much better than everyone else in the class | She does not want any friends at all | She is angry with the whole class
exercise: In a board game the first player has a big advantage. How can you make a series of games fair? | Take turns going first in each game | Let the best player always go first, every time | Let the youngest player always go first | Decide by who arrived at the table first
exercise: A boy cheats and wins a race. Why might his win feel empty? | He does not know if he would have won fairly | Because winning is never fun at all | Because cheating makes your legs much more tired than running | Because winners always feel bad
exercise: Your team wants to pick only the fastest runners for a relay. A slower girl wants to try. What is a fair and clever idea? | Let everyone run a practice and choose by times | Pick only the people who are your friends | Tell her she will slow the team down | Let her run only if nobody else wants to
exercise: A classmate says «Sorry, but you're too sensitive». Why is this not a real apology? | It blames the other person instead of owning up | It is too polite to count as an apology | Apologies must always be written down | It is fine because it starts with sorry
exercise: Why can sharing your toys make playtime more fun for you, not only for others? | Others want to play with you, and games with friends last longer | Shared toys always become new toys | Sharing means you get to keep their toys afterwards, so you end up with more | It only makes it fun for the others
exercise: Someone was rude to you, but you think they are having a bad day. Does that mean you must accept rudeness forever? | No, you can be kind now and still say if it keeps happening | Yes, you must always forgive without saying anything | No, you should be rude back so it is even | Yes, a bad day excuses anything at all
test: Max gets extra time on tests because reading is hard for him. Sara says it is not fair. What is the best answer? | Fair means everyone gets a real chance, and extra time gives him one | Sara is right, everyone must always have exactly the same time, whatever their needs | It is fair only because the teacher likes him | It is unfair, but rules are rules
test: Two brothers in Houston keep fighting over who gets the bigger half of a sandwich. What simple rule solves it? | One cuts, the other picks his half first | Mum always gives the bigger half to the younger | The one who complains less gets the bigger half | They take turns eating bites from the same half
test: A girl's grandpa says: «Don't tell Grandma about the necklace I bought her, I'll give it to her on Sunday». What kind of secret is this? | A surprise, because it will be told soon and make someone happy | A bad secret, because she is told to keep quiet | A dangerous secret that must be told to a teacher straight away, the same day | A secret she must keep forever
test: A coach shouts at the team after a hard loss, then says sorry the next day. What is the most sensible view? | He was upset by the loss and made a mistake he has owned up to | He must hate the team and should be avoided | Shouting is fine as long as you lose | His sorry means the team did lose the match on purpose and he has now found out
test: In a class of 30, only 10 children get to choose the games every week. What would most likely happen? | The other 20 would start to feel left out and lose interest | Everyone would enjoy the games just as much | The 10 would always choose games that suit everyone in the class | The class would play far better together
test: Your friend tells you a secret: she is planning a funny poster for the teacher's birthday. Another friend tells you he is being hit at home and makes you promise not to tell. What do you do? | Keep the first secret and tell a trusted adult about the second | Keep both, because promises must never be broken, whatever the secret is | Tell both, because all secrets are bad | Keep the second and tell the first
test: A player says «Let's share the ball so everyone gets to kick». What is the cleverest reason this helps the game? | When everyone gets a go, everyone wants to keep playing | It means the best player scores even more goals than before | It lets the game finish faster | It makes the ball last longer
test: Why is it smart to wonder «what might be going on for them?» when someone acts strangely? | It helps you respond calmly instead of making things worse | It means you always forgive everything | It helps you find a clever way to get back at them later | It proves that they are always right
test: A boy says he did not cheat because he only looked at the answers «a little». What is the flaw in his thinking? | Any looking makes the result unfair to the others | Looking a little is allowed in every test | Cheating only counts if you copy every single answer | It is fine if nobody else noticed
test: new kid never talks to anyone, prob thinks hes better than us | He may be shy or new, not unfriendly | He must be stuck up and rude | He clearly does not like any of you | He is quiet because he is angry
test: last slice of cake, me n sis fighting. mum says one cuts other picks. why | The cutter will cut fairly so they get a fair piece | So the oldest gets the bigger piece | So mum does not have to help | So the cutter always wins the big piece and gets to choose first
test: dad's been grumpy all wk, did i do something?? | Maybe not, tired or worried people get grumpy | Yes, grumpy people are always cross at you | Yes, he must be angry with you | No, grumpy people never have a reason
test: he won but he peeked at the cards lol. so hes better? | No, winning by cheating shows nothing | Yes, a win is a win, no matter how you got it | Yes, peeking takes real skill and fast eyes | Only if nobody at the table saw him peek
test: everyone's saying Sam stole money, should i pass it on | No, it may be false and could hurt Sam badly | Yes, if lots of people say it, it must be true | Yes, but only to friends | Yes, it is just news
test: this guy knows my school and my dog's name so he must be legit | Not true, he may have read it online | Yes, only friends know that | Yes, so meet him | Yes, so give him your address
test: Ava says if a grown-up says it, it must be true. right? | No, adults can be wrong or lie too | Yes, adults never lie | Yes, they are older | Yes, unless they are strangers
test: everyone's saying Harry's grumpy cos he thinks he's better than us | Maybe not; tired, worried or sad people get grumpy, so do not spread it | It must be true, grumpy means stuck-up | It proves he does not like anyone in the class | It means you should ignore him forever, and get everyone else to ignore him too
test: heard something juicy about Sienna, it's true tho so it's fine to tell everyone right | Not fine; even a true rumour can hurt, so keep it to yourself | Yes, true things can always be shared | Yes, if you say you heard it from others | Yes, as long as she does not find out
test: kid in class says the quiet new boy is stuck up and hates everyone | He may just be shy or new, not unfriendly, so do not spread it | It is true, quiet people are stuck up | It is true, he never says hello | It is true, new kids always think they are better than the kids who were there first
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises fairness? | Each person receiving what he needs, which is not always the same thing | Each person invariably receiving an identical share | The oldest person receiving the most | The loudest person deciding the outcome
exam: Identify the reason for which the arrangement «one cuts, the other chooses» tends to produce fair shares. | The person cutting has a reason to cut fairly | The person choosing always takes the smaller piece | The cutting is invariably carried out by an adult | The pieces are subsequently weighed on a scale
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning rumours. | A rumour may be untrue, and even a true rumour may cause hurt | A rumour is always true if many persons repeat it, since so many cannot be mistaken | A rumour never causes harm | A true rumour must always be shared
exam: It being given that a person's actions are observed, determine what may be known of them. | What the person does, but not always why | Both what the person does and always the reason why | Nothing whatsoever about the person concerned | Only the person's private thoughts and feelings
